    :hasEvidenceText "A non-grid data can be represented as a graph G = { V, E } and a set of features h , where V and E denote the nodes and the edges, respectively."^^xsd:string,
        "A { 0 , 1 } n n denotes the adjacency matrix of G , with each element A ij = 1 indicating there exists an edge between v i and v j"^^xsd:string,
        "Thus, we can also denote a graph as G = ( A , X ) ."^^xsd:string,
        "graphs contain both features and topological structures"^^xsd:string,
        "how to restore the positional and structural information (e.g., local connectivity) of the graph becomes a central issue for Transformer-based models."^^xsd:string,
        "real-world graphs that often have the skewed degree distribution, suffering from the severe sparsity problem"^^xsd:string,
        "the subgraph G corresponding to the receptive field of node as the input, which is generally much smaller than the whole graph."^^xsd:string,
        "we can also denote a graph as G = ( A , X )"^^xsd:string ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :entity,
        owl:Thing ;
    skos:skos_definition "A data structure denoted as G = ( A , X ) consisting of nodes and edges, often described by an adjacency matrix."^^xsd:string ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom "CQ_S0_R1_0006"^^xsd:string,
