    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2202_08235_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "MERIT [52] leverages graph diffusion , edge perturbation (dropping) , subgraph sampling , and feature masking to generate augmented graphs. CSSL [133] augments graphs with the edge perturbation (dropping) and node dropping . Recent work SUGRL [78] leverages feature shuing and graph sampling , S 3 -CL uses feature propagation and pseudo labeling to e- ciently perform GCL. Dierent from the aforementioned pre-dened GraphDA strategies, another line of research proposes to perform data augmentation o" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2202_08235_rag__chunk_021> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2202.08235_rag::chunk_021" ;
    kg:docId "2202.08235_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2202_08235_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "between data samples. Most graph data augmentation methods adopt arbitrary augmentations on the input graph, which may unexpectedly change both structural and semantic patterns of the graph [81]. For example, dropping a carbon atom from the phenyl ring of aspirin breaks the aromatic system and results in a alkene chain, which is an entirely dierent chemical compound. Hence, proposing a label-consistent/semantic-preserving GraphDA method is of necessity. To this end, recent studies [64; 117; 130]" .

