    :hasEvidenceText "A GNN learns a representation for every node in the graph using a set of stacked graph convolution layers."^^xsd:string,
        "Common inference acceleration techniques like pruning ( Zhou et al. , 2021 ) and quantization ( Tailor et al. , 2021 ; Zhao et al. , 2020 ) can speed up GNNs"^^xsd:string,
        "Despite the empirical success of GNNs, there has been relatively little work that mathematically studies their properties."^^xsd:string,
        "Despite the recent success of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), training GNNs on large graphs remains challenging."^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs can take nongird data as inputs. A non-grid data can be represented as a graph G = { V, E } and a set of features h"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs dealing with non-grid data within a discrete space"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs dealing with non-grid data within a discrete space."^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs effectively learn node representations using two components in graph data"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs effectively learn node representations using two components in graph data: they process node features through distributed node-wise transformation"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs in learning node representations from the local neighborhood"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs leverage graph structure, which in practice, allows them to make more accurate predictions"^^xsd:string,
        "GNNs scale to large graphs by minibatch training and subsample node neighbors to deal with high degree nodes."^^xsd:string,
        "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are the predominant technique for learning over graphs."^^xsd:string,
        "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are the rst choice methods for graph machine learning problems thanks to their ability to learn state-of-the-art level representations from graph-structured data."^^xsd:string,
        "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), which can learn effective representations from irregular data"^^xsd:string,
        "Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), which can learn effective representations from irregular data, have given excellent performances in various graphbased tasks"^^xsd:string,
        "Graph Neural Networks. The early GNNs generalize convolution nets to graphs"^^xsd:string,
        "Graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance of node classification."^^xsd:string,
        "Graph neural networks (GNNs) have recently achieved state-of-the-art performance in many graph-based applications. Despite the high performance, they typically need to perform expensive recursive neighborhood expansions"^^xsd:string,
        "Modern GNNs typically adopt a neighborhood aggregation strategy by using 2322 the features and graph structures to learn discriminative node or graph representations."^^xsd:string,
        "Over-smoothing stops GNNs from going deep. FLAG shows its ability to boost both shallow and deep baselines, e.g., GCN and DeeperGCN."^^xsd:string,
        "Recent approaches leverage high-order information from graph structure with GNNs"^^xsd:string,
        "Such graph sparsity inherently limits the effective information propagation and the performance of GNNs"^^xsd:string,
        "The feature extractor can be implemented using any kind of GNNs."^^xsd:string,
        "The recent success of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) [ 53 ] has boosted"^^xsd:string,
        "We formally characterize how expressive different GNN variants are in learning to represent and distinguish between di"^^xsd:string,
        "a shared GNN model denoted as GNN (this could be any GNN model that works for the graph classication task)"^^xsd:string,
        "common GNNs alternately stack graph convolutions, lin"^^xsd:string,
        "it allows to use any GNN architecture with any Aggregate function for the backbone model"^^xsd:string,
        "it causes the GNN to become a predictor for y and not y"^^xsd:string,
        "mainstream GNNs cannot be more powerful than the 1-Weisfeiler-Lehman (WL) isomorphism test"^^xsd:string,
        "methods based on graph neural networks (GNNs) [ 7 , 11 , 29 ] have demonstrated their effectiveness in classifying node labels."^^xsd:string,
        "newer GNN architectures are getting deeper from 64 layers ( Chen et al. , 2020 ) to even 1001 layers"^^xsd:string,
        "the performance degradation caused by large in deep GNNs."^^xsd:string,
        "the total latency explodes quickly as L increases. Figure 1 shows the dependency added by each GNN layer and the exponential explosion of inference time."^^xsd:string,
        "we implement it with GNN, and train it on two down-stream tasks, edge prediction and node classification."^^xsd:string,
        "we propose a universal GNN framework that tackles both the unsupervised graph embedding and the semi-supervised node classification tasks."^^xsd:string ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :entity,
        owl:Thing ;
    skos:skos_definition "Graph Neural Networks that typically adopt a neighborhood aggregation strategy by using the features and graph structures to learn discriminative node or graph representations."^^xsd:string ;
